"""Single source of truth for SDK version. Bump on every src change.
Per repo CLAUDE.md: also update LATEST_SDK_VERSIONS['onelo-python'] in
frontend/lib/sdk-version.ts (Task 12).

Note: Python uses PEP 440 versioning (not semver suffixes). The `0.1.0a1`
form is the PEP 440 equivalent of "alpha 1 / staging" — sorts before
`0.1.0` final and is accepted by hatchling/pip. The `-staging` suffix used
in JS/Swift/Android SDKs is invalid here.
"""
# IMPORTANT: keep the inline comment free of double-quotes — the Publish SDK
# workflow extracts the FIRST quoted string on this line as the version.
__version__ = "0.6.0a4"  # was 0.6.0a3; #739: identify() clears the previous identity snapshot, re-arms ready() and sends since_version=0; stale stream/poll answers for the previous identity are dropped. (a3 was: #819: README says when require_email_verified passes (Email verification on in Auth -> Sign-up); the backend now reports email_verified, so the option no longer refuses everyone. No code change. (a2 was: #368: the Features Event Stream staleness clock is now reset by the server keepalive comment -- httpx_sse drops comments in its decoder, so a healthy idle stream was force-reconnected about every two minutes. (a1 was: #520: per-user Features calls (features.for_user(), identify(user_id)) now raise ValueError when the client holds a publishable key -- use the secret key on the server. Minor bump: behaviour change for pk-configured servers. (a44 was: #105 / ADR-0003: /api/sdk/features/poll now sends its credential as the `X-Publishable-Key` request header instead of the `?key=` query parameter -- the same move a42 made for the stream. /poll fires on a timer, so the raw key (a Secret Key on a server) was written to the reverse proxy access.log on every tick; from 2027-03-31 the backend refuses `?key=` outright. Sent ONLY as the header, never both (the backend resolves `?key=` first and would re-arm Deprecation/Sunset). (a43 was: #100 / ADR-0006: the package now declares its own test environment (a `dev` extra) and its declared httpx range is tested at BOTH ends in CI, instead of borrowing backend/venv -- which shadows src/ with a stale installed copy and pins httpx==0.27.2, the very floor of the `httpx>=0.27,<1.0` this package advertises. Two monitor tests asserted a byte substring of the serialized body and therefore only passed on httpx >= 0.28 (0.28 changed encode_json to compact separators); they now parse the JSON and assert on values, correct across the whole range. Runtime code is UNCHANGED -- serialization stays delegated to httpx, because the byte shape is not a contract and forcing compact separators would hard-code a wire detail to satisfy a test. (a42 was: ADR-0003: the Features Event Stream now sends its credential as the `X-Publishable-Key` request header instead of the `?key=` query parameter. The raw key no longer lands in any reverse proxy access.log on connect, and because the backend (#92) does not stamp Deprecation/Sunset on a header-authenticated connection, the stream stops carrying the Sunset: 2027-03-31 warning it has had since #58. The key is sent ONLY as the header -- sending both would re-arm those headers, since the backend resolves ?key= first. No Stream Token: per ADR-0003 that stays scoped to browser onelo-js, the one client whose native EventSource cannot set a header. Stream behaviour is otherwise untouched -- multiplexing, heartbeat/keepalive handling, the staleness healthcheck, full-jitter backoff, and the terminal 401/403 stop all unchanged. /api/sdk/features/poll is NOT migrated here (#82). (a41 was: BREAKING (config): api_url no longer defaults to https://app.onelo.tools -- a host that has never existed, so every caller relying on the default was silently pointed at nothing (DNS does not resolve; curl cannot connect). api_url is now REQUIRED on Onelo() and on monitor.init(publishable_key=...); both raise ValueError at init with an actionable message naming https://api.onelo.tools, matching the Node and PHP SDKs which already require it. Nothing that previously worked breaks -- callers passing api_url explicitly (the dashboard snippet, backend/app/monitor_setup.py) are unaffected, and anyone on the old default was already broken. Also fixed: pyproject Homepage and 4 README references pointed at the same dead host, and the FastAPI install command was a mangled duplicate that could not be copy-pasted. Enforces CLAUDE.md apiUrl is always required + memory project_canonical_domains, which already documents app.onelo.tools as non-existent. (a40 was: (M8) monitor.init() now auto-emits one unconditional session_opened event (ok=True, source=event, feature_name=session_opened) right after the transport comes up and before any application code runs -- fixes auth/feature-flag-gated apps showing zero monitor events on a cold start even when every instrumented call is correct. Exempt from sample_rate/success_sample_rate (bypass_sampling on _emit) since a randomly-dropped baseline event would defeat its purpose. Not re-emitted on os.register_at_fork's reinit_after_fork -- session == the process's init() lifetime, a forked child only rebuilds the dead transport thread it inherited, it is not a second init(); re-emitting per fork would produce N duplicate rows per process tree. Mirrors packages/onelo-js/src/monitor/monitor.ts's session_opened push. (a39 was: auth polish.) Prior context -- (A6) verify_token/_sync now reject a 200 response with no usable user id (malformed backend response no longer passes as an authenticated empty user). (A4) accept_query_token parity — the ASGI/WSGI middlewares, Flask decorators, Django DRF auth class, and Litestar guard now support authenticating via ?token= (opt-in, default off) for SSE/EventSource clients that can't set an Authorization header; previously only FastAPI. (A5 negative-caching deferred — needs a cache-Protocol extension for a low-severity gain; better designed into @onelo/node.) (a38 was: auth A1/A2/A3.) Prior context — auth backend hardening. (A1) the universal ASGI/WSGI middleware now distinguishes 'backend unreachable' from 'no/invalid token': on OneloAuthUnavailable it sets scope['onelo_auth_unavailable']/environ['onelo.auth_unavailable']=True (user still None) so an Onelo outage can't silently degrade to anonymous access. (A2) verify_token/_sync now reuse a pooled keep-alive httpx client (no TLS handshake per verification) — ephemeral only when a test transport is injected. (A3) a 429 maps to new OneloAuthRateLimited (subclass of Unavailable, carries retry_after) and the retry loops no longer retry-storm a rate-limit. Status mapping unified in _map_response so async/sync can't drift. (a37 was: monitor event_ts on the wire.) Prior context — monitor events now send the real event time on the wire (`ts`, ISO-8601 UTC captured at MonitorEvent creation) so a batched/retried/atexit-drained event isn't mis-dated to ingest time (M7). Backend stores it as a display-only, clamped `event_ts` (dashboard shows event_ts ?? created_at); created_at stays server-now() and remains authoritative for all windowing/quota/incident/retention/cursor. (a36 was: monitor sampling + quota self-throttle + drop signal.) Prior context — (M4) client-side sampling via monitor.init(sample_rate=, success_sample_rate=) — errors vs successes sampled independently, default 1.0 (no change), decided before scope/scrub/transport so an error storm can't thrash the buffer. (M5) transport now reads X-Onelo-Quota-Remaining and self-throttles: preemptive hold-off when remaining hits 0, info log when low, quota_remaining exposed. (M6) dropped-event count is now surfaced via a rate-limited warning (once per newly-dropped batch) instead of climbing silently. (a35 was: monitor fork-safety + release/env placement + WSGI streaming.) Prior context — (M1) transport thread is now rebuilt after fork via os.register_at_fork -> MonitorTransport.reinit_after_fork, so gunicorn/uwsgi preload workers actually deliver events instead of buffering-then-losing them. (M2) release/environment are now stamped onto meta.app.version + meta.environment (the paths the backend aggregates on) not only meta.tags.*, so the dashboard Release/Environment filters populate for Python events. (M3) the WSGI middleware streams the response body (generator) instead of list()-materialising it, fixing broken SSE/streaming/large downloads. (a34 was: _UserSnapshotCache bounded.) Prior context — _UserSnapshotCache (for_user) is now bounded — expired entries are deleted on the get() that finds them (were leaking until the same user returned), and put() enforces a hard cap (_USER_SNAPSHOT_MAX_ENTRIES=10000) by purging expired then evicting soonest-to-expire. Fixes an unbounded memory growth on backends resolving a long tail of one-shot user_ids. (a33 was: Feature surfaces upsell metadata; cache stores full wire state.) Prior context — Feature now surfaces the backend's upsell metadata (reason, required_plan, required_plan_label, upgrade_cta + computed upgrade_hint) so server-rendered apps can build an Available-in-Pro CTA. The feature cache stores the FULL wire state dict per feature (was status-only); SSE/poll/for_user keep it end-to-end; snapshot() still returns status-only for the monitor flag provider. Back-compat: replace_all normalises a bare status string to {status: str}. (a32 was: is_visible fail-closed for unknown status + is_known; _after_fork propagates feature_environment; polling handles up_to_date + discovery_requested.)))))))
